 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is $O(N)$ per pixel, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Training time depends on MLP size and densification steps.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for Gaussian attributes plus $O(M)$ for MLP weights, where $M$ is the number of MLP parameters.
-*   **Strengths:** High-fidelity full-body reconstruction; supports arbitrary SMPL-X poses.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires SMPL-X fitting; MLP inference adds latency compared to rigid transforms.
-*   **When to use:** When full-body animation with high visual fidelity is required.
-*   **When not to use:** For head-only avatars (use Gaussian Avatars) or when computational resources are extremely limited.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is comparable to standard 3DGS but requires additional forward passes through the deformation MLP per Gaussian per frame. Inference is fast, dominated by splatting rasterization.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Higher than static 3DGS due to the storage of the deformation MLP weights and the need to store pose-conditioned parameters.
+*   **Strengths:** High-fidelity full-body reconstruction; handles both rigid and non-rigid motion; real-time rendering capability.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires multi-view video for training; performance degrades with occluded body parts; complex to implement due to the integration of SMPL-X and MLP [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-3DGS-Avatar was introduced as a method to reconstruct full-body avatars using 3D Gaussian Splatting [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the SMPL-X parametric model and introduces per-Gaussian deformation MLPs to handle non-rigid body dynamics [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the broader family of Gaussian Avatar Reconstruction methods [S2, unofficial].
+3DGS-Avatar was introduced as an advancement in human-centric Gaussian Splatting, building on the principles of SMPL-based deformation. It specifically addresses the limitations of rigid SMPL skinning by introducing per-Gaussian deformation networks, allowing for more realistic soft-tissue dynamics [S1, unofficial]. It is part of a broader family of methods including Gaussian Avatar and Animatable Gaussians, which also leverage parametric models for avatar reconstruction [S2, unofficial].
 
